{"description":"`LoadOrBuildWithOptions` decided cache freshness by running the whole build: it called `buildWithOptions`, compared digests against the cached index, and on a miss built a second time. Because the build reads every file and parses every Go file, a so-called cache hit did all the work the cache exists to avoid and then discarded the result. The cache saved only the final write.","impact":"A second code-context call on an unchanged tree cost as much as the first, so the mechanism was slower than no cache at all while appearing to work. Fixed with `Index.StatKey`, a path/size/mtime fingerprint computed during the same traversal that builds the index: deciding freshness is now one directory walk with no file read and no parse, and a miss builds exactly once. Sharing the traversal with `currentStatKey` makes it impossible for the fingerprint and the builder to disagree about what the tree contains — a hit granted for a tree that would build differently would be a correctness bug, not a performance one."}